---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FigureWithCaption from '../../components/FigureWithCaption.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const projects = (await getCollection('projects')).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf(),
);
const tags = [...new Set(projects.flatMap((p) => p.data.tags))].sort();
const years = [...new Set(projects.map((p) => p.data.date.getUTCFullYear()))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
const languages = [...new Set(projects.map((p) => p.data.language).filter(Boolean))].sort();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="AJ Wildridge’s research at Fermilab: quantum information in top-quark pairs, machine learning for collider reconstruction, and quantum computing. Papers, studies, and interactive tools in one place."
>
  <header class="research-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Physics, methods, and tools</p>
    <h1>From collisions<br />to quantum information.</h1>
    <p class="research-lead">
      I am a postdoctoral researcher at Fermilab. I study the quantum properties of top-quark pairs
      and develop computational methods that help us reconstruct and understand particle collisions.
    </p>
    <nav class="topic-nav" aria-label="Research topics">
      <a href="#top-quark-heading">01 · Quantum information</a>
      <a href="#ml-heading">02 · Machine learning</a>
      <a href="#quantum-heading">03 · Quantum computing</a>
      <a href="#tools">Studies &amp; tools ↓</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <section class="research-topic" aria-labelledby="top-quark-heading">
    <div class="topic-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / Collider quantum information</p>
      <h2 id="top-quark-heading">What can a top-quark pair tell us about its quantum state?</h2>
      <p>
        Top quarks decay before forming hadrons. The directions of their decay products carry
        information about their spins, letting us study the joint quantum state of a top quark and
        an antiquark at the Large Hadron Collider.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work connects CMS measurements of spin correlations and entanglement with
        quantum-information observables. Our steering-geometry study uses ellipsoids to describe the
        conditional spin states of one particle after a measurement of the other, and to study
        sensitivity to physics beyond the Standard Model.
      </p>
      <ul class="research-links">
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/top-quark-polarization-spin-correlations-dilepton-2026/"
            >TOP-23-006 · Dilepton polarization and spin correlations ↗</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/quantum-steering-geometry-at-high-energy-particle-colliders-2026/"
            >Quantum steering geometry · 2026 preprint ↗</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/observation-of-quantum-entanglement-in-top-quark-p-2024/"
            >CMS observation of top-quark entanglement ↗</a
          >
        </li>
        <li><a href="/demos/spin-explorer/">Explore spin correlations interactively →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <FigureWithCaption
      src="/images/research-top-spin.svg"
      alt="Schematic: top and antitop decay directions inform a spin-correlation matrix and the ellipsoid of conditional spin states."
      caption="From decay angles to a joint spin state and its steering geometry. Illustration only; the ellipsoid is not a measured result."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="research-topic" aria-labelledby="ml-heading">
    <div class="topic-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 / Machine learning for collider data</p>
      <h2 id="ml-heading">Reconstruct the event. Learn from what is missing.</h2>
      <p>
        A detector records the products of a collision, while the underlying process must be
        reconstructed. In dileptonic top-quark events, two unobserved neutrinos make that
        reconstruction especially challenging.
      </p>
      <p>
        I work on transformer-based approaches to this problem, including Bumblebee: a foundation
        model trained on masked particle four-vectors. Related work studies supervised event
        reconstruction and the design of anomaly-detection challenges across scientific disciplines.
      </p>
      <ul class="research-links">
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/bumblebee-foundation-model-for-particle-physics-di-2024/"
            >Bumblebee foundation model ↗</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/enhanced-reconstruction-of-dileptonic-top-quark-an-2025/"
            >CMS note on dileptonic top reconstruction ↗</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/building-machine-learning-challenges-for-anomaly-d-2025/"
            >Anomaly-detection challenges in science ↗</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <FigureWithCaption
      src="/images/ml-model-card.svg"
      alt="Particle four-vectors, including a masked entry, pass through a transformer to support event reconstruction and downstream classification."
      caption="A schematic of representation learning for collision events. Reconstruction and classification require their own validation; this is not a performance plot."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="research-topic" aria-labelledby="quantum-heading">
    <div class="topic-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 / Quantum computing for reconstruction</p>
      <h2 id="quantum-heading">Which tracks came from the same collision?</h2>
      <p>
        Several proton collisions can occur in the same bunch crossing. Primary-vertex
        reconstruction groups charged-particle tracks according to where they originated.
      </p>
      <p>
        My quantum-annealing work encodes this assignment as an optimization problem and tests small
        instances on artificial collider events. The study examines embedding, annealing settings,
        and comparisons with simulated annealing, including the limits imposed by hardware size and
        connectivity.
      </p>
      <ul class="research-links">
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/track-clustering-with-a-quantum-annealer-for-prima-2019/"
            >Track clustering with a quantum annealer ↗</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/quantum-annealing-vertexing/">Read about the reconstruction study →</a>
        </li>
        <li><a href="/demos/pegasus-embedder/">Explore graph embedding on Pegasus →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <FigureWithCaption
      src="/images/quantum-workflow.svg"
      alt="Tracks from two vertices are encoded as binary assignments, mapped to a quantum annealer, and decoded into track groups."
      caption="The path from track compatibility to a vertex assignment. This conceptual diagram does not imply a speedup over classical reconstruction."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="tools" class="research-tools" aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Explore the work</p>
    <h2 id="tools-heading">Studies &amp; interactive tools</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl">
      Publication-linked studies sit alongside browser demonstrations. Each entry explains what it
      does and links to the paper, code, or demo.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5 text-sm" id="project-count" aria-live="polite">
      Showing {projects.length} of {projects.length} studies and tools
    </p>
    <form
      id="project-filters"
      role="search"
      aria-label="Research filters"
      class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4"
      method="get"
    >
      <label
        ><span class="block text-sm mb-1">Search</span><input
          class="w-full border rounded px-3 py-2"
          type="search"
          name="q"
          placeholder="Search research and tools"
        /></label
      >
      <label
        ><span class="block text-sm mb-1">Tag</span><select
          class="w-full border rounded px-3 py-2"
          name="tag"
          ><option value="">All</option>{
            tags.map((tag) => <option value={tag}>{tag}</option>)
          }</select
        ></label
      >
      <label
        ><span class="block text-sm mb-1">Year</span><select
          class="w-full border rounded px-3 py-2"
          name="year"
          ><option value="">All</option>{
            years.map((year) => <option value={year}>{year}</option>)
          }</select
        ></label
      >
      <label
        ><span class="block text-sm mb-1">Language</span><select
          class="w-full border rounded px-3 py-2"
          name="language"
          ><option value="">All</option>{
            languages.map((language) => <option value={language}>{language}</option>)
          }</select
        ></label
      >
      <div class="sm:col-span-2 lg:col-span-4 flex gap-4 items-center">
        <button type="submit" class="button-primary">Apply filters</button><a
          href="/research/#tools"
          class="text-link">Clear filters</a
        >
      </div>
    </form>
    <div id="project-list" class="mt-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2" data-label="studies and tools">
      {
        projects.map(({ id, data: project }) => (
          <a
            class="block border rounded p-6 hover:shadow-sm"
            href={`/research/${id}/`}
            data-filter-item
            data-search={`${project.title} ${project.summary || ''}`}
            data-tags={JSON.stringify(project.tags)}
            data-year={project.date.getUTCFullYear()}
            data-language={project.language || ''}
          >
            <p class="eyebrow mb-3">
              {project.date.getUTCFullYear()} · {project.language || 'Research'}
            </p>
            <>
              <h3 class="text-xl">{project.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">{project.summary}</p>
            </>
            <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">{project.tags.join(' · ')}</p>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8" data-filter-empty hidden>
      No studies or tools match these filters. Try another search or clear the filters.
    </p>
    <script>
      import { enhanceFilters } from '../../lib/filter-list';
      enhanceFilters(
        document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('#project-filters')!,
        document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#project-list')!,
        document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#project-count')!,
      );
    </script>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .research-intro {
    max-width: 62rem;
    padding: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .research-intro h1 {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .research-lead {
    max-width: 45rem;
    color: rgb(var(--color-muted));
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }
  .topic-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
  .topic-nav a {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .research-topic {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 3.5rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid rgb(var(--color-border));
  }
  .topic-copy h2 {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-family: Georgia, serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.3rem);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .topic-copy > p:not(.eyebrow) {
    margin: 1rem 0;
    color: rgb(var(--color-muted));
  }
  .research-links {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .research-links a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    color: rgb(var(--color-accent));
  }
  .research-tools {
    border-top: 1px solid rgb(var(--color-border));
    padding: 3rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .research-tools h2 {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 960px) {
    .research-topic {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 3rem;
    }
  }
</style>
